hypotheses.statement:
If no sequence in [def:prohibited-sequence](#def:prohibited-sequence) exists, then for some $\varepsilon>0$ and $C<\infty$, $$\label{critical:eq:angle-budget}
 \int_{k_0}^K\mu_f(k)^2\,\,\mathrm dk\le C(1+K)^{2-\varepsilon}
 \qquad(K\ge k_0).$$

For the deterministic level in [thm:row-sign](#thm:row-sign), put $$\label{sign:h1-radius}
 h_1=\tfrac12K^{\alpha/2},\qquad
 r_{\mathrm{rob}}=e^{-k-h_1/4}.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Fix an integer $m\ge2$, a bound $M$, and put $p=m+2$, $r_0=m+15$. There are $R,K,N$, depending only on $M,m$, with the following properties. Suppose $0\le\sigma\le1$, $u$ is real symmetric, and $\|u\|_{r_0,\sigma}\le M$. For every integer $q\ge N$, let $\delta=2\pi/q$. There is a stationary fixed-endpoint mesh $$\label{ana:mesh-definition}
X_j(x)=x+j\delta+w_j(x),\qquad 0\le j\le q,\qquad
w_0=w_q=0,$$ on $\mathcal A_{\sigma'}$, where $\sigma'=(1-K/q^2)\sigma$, satisfying $$\label{ana:mesh-norm}
\|w\|_*:=
q^2\max_j\|w_j\|_{p,\sigma'}
+q^3\max_j\|w_{j+1}-w_j\|_{p,\sigma'}\le R.$$ It depends differentiably on smooth real variations of $u$, and $$\|\dot w\|_*\le C\|\dot u\|_{r_0,\sigma}.$$ The analogous difference estimate holds for two inputs in this ball. On the real axis, the mesh is also unique pointwise in the ball with the same scaled position and step bounds. This real uniqueness ball may be enlarged by any fixed amount if $N$ is increased.

For each integer $m\ge2$ and $M<\infty$, take $r_0=m+15$. There are constants $K,N$, independent of $0\le\sigma\le1$, such that, whenever $\|u\|_{r_0,\sigma}\le M$ and $|n|\ge N$, the derivative of [ana:constraint-definition](#ana:constraint-definition) has the form $$\label{ana:constraint-derivative}
(\,\mathrm dC_n)_u(v)=[b_n(u)v]_n.$$ Here $$\label{ana:coefficient-estimate}
\|b_n(u)-1\|_{m,(1-K/n^2)\sigma}\le\frac{K}{|n|},$$ and, for two such inputs on the same strip, $$\label{ana:coefficient-lipschitz}
\|b_n(u)-b_n(\widetilde u)\|_{m,(1-K/n^2)\sigma}
\le\frac{K}{|n|}\|u-\widetilde u\|_{r_0,\sigma}.$$ The derivative identity holds for smooth real variations, and for real-symmetric holomorphic variations with the indicated finite norms.

For the function $u$ associated with the actual table by [ana:normalization](#ana:normalization), $$\label{ana:constraint-vanishing}
C_n(u)=0
\qquad\text{for all sufficiently large }|n|.$$

Fix the bounds in [prop:high-frequency-estimate](#prop:high-frequency-estimate) for $m=2$, and increase $N$ if necessary. On the Wiener space of width $\sigma$, the coefficient formula for $\,\mathrm dC(u)$ defines a bounded operator $A(u)$ satisfying $$\label{ana:wiener-near-identity}
A(u)=\Pi_{\ge N}+R(u),\qquad \|R(u)\|\le\frac{C}{N}.$$ For two bounded inputs on the same strip, $$\label{ana:wiener-lipschitz}
\|A(u)-A(\widetilde u)\|
\le C\|u-\widetilde u\|_{r_0,\sigma}.$$ In particular, the restriction $A_H(u)$ to high input modes has inverse of norm at most $2$ when $N$ is sufficiently large. All these operators preserve real data.

There are constants $C,B_0$, depending only on the fixed action domain and the fixed smallness bounds above, with the following property. Suppose $U$ is real-symmetric, even in $t$, and $U-y$ is periodic, and $$\|E(U)\|_{\mathcal D^1_0}\le\varepsilon<1.$$ Let $N\ge1$ satisfy $e^{-N\delta}\le\varepsilon$, and put $Q=1+N+\delta^{-1}$. If $CQ^{B_0}\varepsilon$ is sufficiently small, there is a real-symmetric, even correction $\Delta U$, periodic in $y$, such that $$\begin{aligned}
 \max_{0\le j\le3}\|\partial_y^j\Delta U\|_{\mathcal D^0_{20}}
     &\le CQ^{B_0}\varepsilon,\label{joint:step-correction}\\
 \|E(U+\Delta U)\|_{\mathcal D^1_{20}}
     &\le CQ^{B_0}\varepsilon^2.
 \label{joint:step-error}
\end{aligned}$$ The exponent $B_0$ is fixed; it is independent of $N$, the retained rationals, and the order of any formal approximation used to initialize the iteration.

Under the hypotheses of [prop:joint-newton](#prop:joint-newton), let $t_0=2\pi l/q\ne0$ be reduced, with $q\le N$ and $|t_0|<d-2\delta$. There are holomorphic jets $w,\dot w$ on $Y_{10}$, real-symmetric in $y$, such that $$\begin{aligned}
 E+\partial(a\partial w)&=O(P\varepsilon^2),\notag\\
 \bigl(E+\partial(a\partial w)\bigr)^{\displaystyle\cdot}
       &=O(P\varepsilon^2),\label{joint:rational-compatibility}\\
 w,\dot w&=O(P\varepsilon).\notag
\end{aligned}$$ The dot differentiates the entire expression with respect to $t$ at $t_0$, using the displayed assigned jet for $w$. The bounds also hold after any of the fixed derivative losses used below.

Let $\nu\in\{1/2,1\}$, and suppose a mean-zero periodic function $F$ is holomorphic on $\mathcal D_{j_0}^\nu$, with norm at most $M$. Suppose, for $1\le |n|\le N$, its values at the divisor zeros $0<|t_j|<d-b\delta$, where $b\ge j_0+1$, satisfy $$|F_n(t_j)|\le\eta e^{-|n|(s-a\delta)},
 \qquad a\ge j_0.$$ Define, for these retained modes, $$\begin{aligned}
 R_n(t)&=\sum_{0<|t_j|<d-b\delta}F_n(t_j)
       \frac{\sin(n(t-t_j)/2)}{n(t-t_j)/2},\label{joint:sinc-interpolant}\\
 H_n(t)&=\frac{F_n(t)-R_n(t)}{d_n(t)},
 \label{joint:divided-coefficient}
\end{aligned}$$ and put $H_0=0$, $H_n=0$ for $|n|>N$. On $|t|<d-(b+1)\delta$, every retained $H_n$ is holomorphic. For an integer $J$ larger than $a$, $j_0$, and $b+1$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \|H\|_{\mathcal D_J^{\nu-1/2}}&\le P(M+\eta),\notag\\
 \|\partial H-F\|_{\mathcal D_J^\nu}
       &\le P\bigl(\eta+Me^{-N\delta}\bigr).
 \label{joint:division-bounds}
\end{aligned}$$ Fixed derivatives are bounded after fixed additional domain losses by increasing $P$. Real symmetry and evenness in $t$ are preserved.

Put $H=h_X(0)=h_L(0)$. The parametrization $\Gamma(y)=X(y,0)$ is meromorphic on $$D_H=\{y\in\mathbb C:|\Im y|<H\}.$$ If $H<\infty$, it has no meromorphic continuation patch through any point of either boundary line of $D_H$. Moreover, on its meromorphic domain, $$\frac{\det(\Gamma',\Gamma'')^4}
      {(\Gamma'\cdot\Gamma')^3}=c^2,
 \label{cont:intrinsic-normalization}$$ where $c\ne0$ is the normalized curvature constant from the glancing construction.

Suppose that $a$ has a zero of order $n\geq1$ at $p\in D_H$. Then $n\ne3$. Set $$D_+=n+3,\qquad D_-=3-n.$$ There is a germ $\xi(w)=w+O(1)$ at infinity such that both $\xi^{D_+}$ and $\xi^{D_-}$ are rational. It satisfies $$\frac{\xi(w+1)^{D_+}-\xi(w)^{D_+}}
      {\xi(w+1)^{D_-}-\xi(w)^{D_-}}
 \frac{\xi(w-1)^{D_+}-\xi(w)^{D_+}}
      {\xi(w-1)^{D_-}-\xi(w)^{D_-}}
 =\left(\frac{D_+}{D_-}\right)^2
      \xi(w)^{2(D_+-D_-)}.
 \label{shape:profile-reflection}$$ The corresponding assertion for a pole holds after interchanging $Z$ and $W$.

Let $Q$ be holomorphic on a fixed neighborhood of an integration segment from $0$ to $J$. Regard $Q$, $J$, and the initial data $F(0),F'(0)$ for $F''=QF$ as variables. Near zero initial data and a pair $(Q,J)$ for which $k_0(J)\ne0$, the right side of <a href="#cone:kernel-action" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="cone:kernel-action">[cone:kernel-action]</a>, with the square root equal to $k_0(J)$ at zero initial data, extends holomorphically through $F(0)F(J)=0$. This extension is even in $F$. Its bounds and analytic dependence, including derivatives on smaller neighborhoods, are uniform on compact sets of admissible $(Q,J)$.*


*In particular, for $J$ in a fixed sufficiently small neighborhood of $1$, and small $Q,F(0),F'(0)$, $$\label{cone:small-action}
 p-\frac{J^3}{24}
 =O\left(\|Q\|+|F(0)|^2+|F'(0)|^2\right).$$ The same estimate holds for any fixed number of $J$-derivatives on a smaller neighborhood.

Let $d_n\to0$, $b_n\ge1$, and $x_n\in\mathbb R$. Suppose, on a sufficiently large fixed cone starting at depth $d_n$, that $$\label{cone:coarse-bound}
 \frac{d_n}{b_n}M(s)\le C(d_n/\delta_s)^\alpha,
 \qquad \frac12<\alpha<1.$$ The following statements hold; whenever an arbitrary fixed aperture is used, the hypothesis is required on its corresponding fixed enlargement.*


1.  *On a strictly coarser cone, starting at a fixed sufficiently large multiple of $d_n$, there is a passage with parameter radius $c(d_n/b_n)(\delta/d_n)^\alpha$, and $$\label{cone:coarse-error}
     |\mathfrak s-y|
     =O\left(b_n^2|t|^2d_n^{2\alpha-2}\delta^{1-2\alpha}\right).$$ Equivalently, for $Y=(y-x_n-\mathrm iH)/d_n$, $S=(\mathfrak s-x_n-\mathrm iH)/d_n$, and $T=tb_n/d_n$, $$|S-Y|\le C|T|^2(-\Im Y)^{1-2\alpha}$$ far enough down at each bounded $T$.*



2.  *Suppose in addition that $$\label{cone:quiet-profile}
     \frac{d_n}{b_n}M(x_n+\mathrm iH+d_n S)\longrightarrow0$$ uniformly on every compact subset of $\Im S<0$. For every $c_1,T_1>0$, lift passages reach depth $c_1d_n$ for all $|t|\le T_1d_n/b_n$, for large $n$. At real parameters, with $\Delta(t)=H-h_X(t)$, $$\label{cone:quiet-width}
     \sup_{|T|\le T_1}\frac{\Delta(Td_n/b_n)}{d_n}\longrightarrow0.$$*

3.  *The hypotheses in <span class="upright">(ii)</span> are impossible if $b_n$ stays bounded. In fact a vanishing weighted cone bound down to a sufficiently small fixed positive depth ratio already gives this contradiction.

Assume the hypotheses in [thm:exponential-obstruction](#thm:exponential-obstruction), without assuming its conclusion. Fix $\alpha\in(1/2,\alpha_0)$ and $T_0<\infty$. There are $D_0$ and $C$, independent of the fixed aperture $A$, with the following property. For every fixed $A<\infty$, and eventually in $n$, there are joint holomorphic vertical passages on compact subsets of $$\label{profile:deep-domain}
 D=-\Im Y>D_0,\qquad |\Re Y|<AD,\qquad |T|<T_0,$$ and $$\label{profile:deep-estimate}
 |S_n(Y,T)-Y|\le C|T|^2D^{1-2\alpha}.$$ These passages start in the actual lift domain and remain in the shape strip. Their eventual sequence threshold may depend on $A$ and on the compact subset.

On the deep domains of [profile:deep-data](#profile:deep-data), the passages converge locally uniformly, with derivatives, to a unique holomorphic model $S_*(Y,T)$. With $\lambda=Tc_0e^{-\mathrm iS_*}$ and $J=\partial_Y S_*$, it satisfies $$\label{profile:energy-equation}
 E_F(\lambda,J)=1/12
 \quad\hbox{or}\quad E_Q(\lambda,J)=1/12,$$ respectively, and $S_*-Y\to0$, $J\to1$ as $-\Im Y\to\infty$ in each fixed cone.

There is an absolute constant $C$ such that the following holds. Let $f$ be holomorphic and not identically zero on the unit disk, suppose $\log|f|\le K$, and suppose $f''/f$ extends holomorphically through every zero. Then $$\label{profile:log-deficit-bound}
 \sup_{|z|<1/4}|f''(z)/f(z)|^{1/2}
 \le C\left(1+\int_{|z|<1}(K-\log|f(z)|)\,\,\mathrm dA(z)\right).$$ On a disk of radius $\Delta$, the corresponding left side is $\Delta\sup|q|^{1/2}$ and the integral on the right is replaced by the disk average of the log deficit.

Under <a href="#critical:eq:envelope" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="critical:eq:envelope">[critical:eq:envelope]</a> and <a href="#critical:eq:first-moment" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="critical:eq:first-moment">[critical:eq:first-moment]</a>, there are constants $c,z_0,C>0$ such that, for real $t$ and $z=|t|N(k)/d_k\le z_0$, $$\label{critical:eq:real-deficit}
 \Delta(t)\le C d_kz^\gamma,
 \qquad \gamma=\frac1{1+a}.$$ Moreover, with $$\label{critical:eq:m-exponent}
 m=\frac{4(2-\gamma)}{4-\gamma}<2,$$ one has $$\label{critical:eq:negative-P}
 \frac{d_kP^-(x+\mathrm i(H-d_k))}{N(k)^2}
 \le C b(x,k)^m.$$ Here $P^-$ is the nonnegative negative part of $P$.

Let $N$ be an envelope as in [critical:def:envelope](#critical:def:envelope), assume the first-moment bound <a href="#critical:eq:first-moment" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="critical:eq:first-moment">[critical:eq:first-moment]</a>, and suppose $\sup_{k\ge k_0}\mu(k)/N(k)<\infty$. Then $$\label{critical:eq:L2-bootstrap}
 \mu(k)\le C N(k),\qquad
 \mathbb E\frac{d_k|P|}{N(k)^2}\le C.$$ There is $\eta>0$, depending only on the fixed envelope constants, such that $$\label{critical:eq:higher-moment}
 \left\|\frac{d_kM}{N(k)}\right\|_{2+\eta}\le C.$$ The estimates for $M$ hold also with suprema on every fixed tile. If $N$ is nondecreasing, then in addition $$\label{critical:eq:global-envelope}
 \sup_x d_kM\le C(1+k)N(k),\qquad
 \sup_x\frac{d_k|P|}{N(k)^2}\le C(1+k)^2.$$

Let $N$ satisfy the hypotheses of [thm:critical-bootstrap](#thm:critical-bootstrap), with fixed constants, and let $d_a=e^{-k_a}$ be a sufficiently small ancestor depth. Let $\omega,\nu$ be as above, with fixed kernel comparison constants. Suppose that for every fixed tile enlargement needed below, $$\label{critical:eq:weighted-coarse}
 \left\|\sup_{s\in\mathcal T_A(j,x)}d_jM(s)\right\|_{L^2(\nu)}
 \le C_A N(j),\qquad k_0\le j\le k_a+3,$$ and that $$\label{critical:eq:weighted-anchor}
 \int |P(x+\mathrm i(H-d_a))|\,\,\mathrm d\omega(x)
 \le C\frac{N(k_a)^2}{d_a}.$$ Then on all finer levels, and with every fixed tile enlargement, $$\label{critical:eq:weighted-conclusion}
 \left\|\sup_{s\in\mathcal T_A(j,x)}d_jM(s)\right\|_{L^2(\nu)}
 \le C'_A N(j).$$ The constants are independent of $k_a$, of $\omega$, and of the finite bootstrap ratio. No higher weighted moment is asserted.

Consider sequences of levels $k_n\to\infty$, scales $d_n=e^{-k_n}$, and amplitudes $B_n=\mu(k_n)\to\infty$. Suppose there are envelopes $N_n$ satisfying the hypotheses of [thm:critical-bootstrap](#thm:critical-bootstrap) with constants independent of $n$, and $N_n(k_n)\le C B_n$.*




*For a fixed $\alpha\in(1/2,1)$ and a sufficiently large fixed aperture $L$, there cannot be centers $x_n$ such that, for every fixed $c>0$, $$\label{critical:eq:quiet-cone}
 \sup_{\substack{c d_n\le\delta\le\delta_b\\
                  |\Re s-x_n|\le L\delta\\
                  \delta=H-\Im s}}
 \frac{d_nM(s)}{B_n}\left(\frac{\delta}{d_n}\right)^\alpha
 \longrightarrow0.$$ In particular, suppose the profiles $$\label{critical:eq:profiles}
 F_n(S)=\frac{d_n}{B_n}f(x_n+\mathrm iH+d_nS),\qquad
 Q_n(S)=\left(\frac{d_n}{B_n}\right)^2q(x_n+\mathrm iH+d_nS)$$ converge to $(0,0)$ on compact subsets of the lower half-plane. They cannot also satisfy, for some fixed $\alpha_0>1/2$ and an aperture $L$ sufficiently large for a chosen exponent $1/2<\alpha<\min(1,\alpha_0)$, $$\label{critical:eq:bounded-coarse-cone}
 \frac{d_n}{B_n}M(s)\le C\left(\frac{d_n}{\delta}\right)^{\alpha_0}
 \quad(d_n\le\delta\le\delta_b,
             \ |\Re s-x_n|\le L\delta).$$ Every compact holomorphic limit of the pair in <a href="#critical:eq:profiles" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="critical:eq:profiles">[critical:eq:profiles]</a> is pure: its product $FQ$ vanishes identically.

Assume that no sequence in [def:prohibited-sequence](#def:prohibited-sequence) exists, and fix $0<\alpha<1/2$. Set $$G_f(j)=\log\max_x|f(x+\mathrm i(H-e^{-j}))|.$$ There are constants $C,c>0$ and a sufficiently large $K_0$ such that every $\kappa\ge K_0$ with $\mu_f(\kappa)\ge\kappa^\alpha$ satisfies $$\label{critical:eq:growth-charge}
 G_f(\kappa+C\log\kappa)-G_f(\kappa-C\log\kappa)
 \ge c\mu_f(\kappa).$$

If no sequence in [def:prohibited-sequence](#def:prohibited-sequence) exists, then for some $\varepsilon>0$ and $C<\infty$, $$\label{critical:eq:angle-budget}
 \int_{k_0}^K\mu_f(k)^2\,\,\mathrm dk\le C(1+K)^{2-\varepsilon}
 \qquad(K\ge k_0).$$ In particular [thm:sequence-exclusion](#thm:sequence-exclusion) will establish this conclusion and [cor:critical-polynomial-bounds](#cor:critical-polynomial-bounds) in the critical case.

After passage to a subsequence, $m_n$ converges locally uniformly on $(0,\infty)$ to a positive function $m$. It satisfies $$\label{model:moment-properties}
 m(1)=1,\qquad m(D)\le D^{-1},\qquad \log m
 \text{ is convex as a function of }D.$$

Fix $c\in(1/2,1)$, and define $$\label{model:contact-envelope}
 \rho_c(x)=cx+\inf_{v\ge x}\bigl(\rho(v)-cv\bigr).$$ A point is called a contact if $\rho(x)=\rho_c(x)$. Exactly one of the following alternatives is needed.*



1.  *There are contacts tending to $+\infty$ at which $p$ is unbounded. In that case there exist $x_j\to\infty$, $P_j=p(x_j)\to
    \infty$, and $D_j=e^{x_j}$ such that $$\label{model:exponential-moment-tangent}
     \frac{m(D_j(1-u/P_j))}{m(D_j)}\longrightarrow e^u$$ uniformly on bounded real $u$-intervals. For every fixed $\alpha_0\in(1/2,c)$, these choices can also satisfy $$\label{model:contact-power-tail}
     \frac{m(D_j(1+v/P_j))}{m(D_j)}
     \le C(1+v)^{-\alpha_0}\qquad(v\ge1),$$ with $C$ independent of $j$.*




2.  *The slopes at all sufficiently large contacts are bounded. There exist $x_j\to\infty$, a locally Lipschitz function $\rho_*$ with $\rho_*(0)=0$, and a finite $\lambda\ge1$ such that $$\label{model:rho-translate-limit}
     \rho(x_j+u)-\rho(x_j)\longrightarrow\rho_*(u)$$ locally uniformly, and $$\label{model:rho-two-ended-slope}
     \rho_*(u)=\lambda u+o(|u|)\qquad(u\to+\infty\text{ or }u\to-\infty).$$ There are constants $C,P'>0$, independent of $j$, for which $$\begin{aligned}
    \label{model:rho-coarse-envelope}
     \rho(v)&\ge \rho(x_j)+c(v-x_j)-C&& (v\ge x_j),\\
    \label{model:rho-fine-envelope}
     \rho(v)&\ge \rho(x_j)-P'(x_j-v)-C&& (v\le x_j).
    \end{aligned}$$ Moreover $\rho(x_j)=O(1+x_j)$.

There are depths $d'\to0$, levels $K=-\log d'\to\infty$, normalizations $B'=\mu(K)\to\infty$, and horizontal centers $\xi_n$ with the following properties. Set $$\label{model:selected-normalization}
 F_n(S)=\frac{d'}{B'}f(\xi_n+\mathrm iH+d'S),\qquad
 Q_n(S)=\left(\frac{d'}{B'}\right)^2q(\xi_n+\mathrm iH+d'S).$$ Then $F_n\to F\not\equiv0$ and $Q_n\to0$ locally uniformly on $\mathbb H_-=\{S:\Im S<0\}$, and $F''/F$ is holomorphic there. The global normalized moment laws converge locally uniformly to $$\label{model:star-moment}
 m_*(D)=\exp\bigl(-\rho_*(\log D)\bigr),\qquad
 \log m_*(D)=-\lambda\log D+o(|\log D|)$$ at both ends of $0<D<\infty$. For fixed $1-c<\beta<1/2$ and some fixed $a>0$, $$\label{model:new-global-envelope}
 \mu(l)\le C B'
 \exp\bigl(\beta(K-l)_++a(l-K)_+\bigr)
 \qquad(l\ge k_0),$$ with $C$ independent of the sequence index. Finally, one can fix $\alpha\in(1/2,1-\beta)$ so that, for every fixed aperture $A$, $$\label{model:selected-cones}
 \max(|F_n(S)|,\sqrt{|Q_n(S)|})
 \le C_A(-\Im S)^{-\alpha}$$ when $1\le-\Im S\le\delta_b/d'$ and $|\Re S|\le A(-\Im S)$, eventually for that aperture.

There is a limiting impact coordinate $S(T,Y)$ with the following properties.*

1.  *For every fixed aperture and every bounded complex parameter range, it is jointly holomorphic sufficiently deep in the corresponding cone and is the locally uniform limit of <a href="#model:phase-scaling" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="model:phase-scaling">[model:phase-scaling]</a>. There, $$\label{model:deep-anchor}
     S(T,Y)-Y=O\bigl(|T|^2D^{1-2\alpha}\bigr),
    \qquad D=-\Im Y\longrightarrow\infty.$$*


2.  *Put $$\label{model:energy}
     J=S_Y,\qquad z=\frac{TF(S)J}{2},\qquad
     g(z)=\frac{3(\sin z-z\cos z)}{z^3},\quad g(0)=1.$$ The limiting equation is $$\label{model:energy-equation}
     J^3g(z)=1.$$*


3.  *For every fixed $\varepsilon>0$, set $r=1/(\lambda+\varepsilon)$. There are constants $C_\varepsilon,t_0>0$ such that, for every real $|T|<t_0$, the model is holomorphic in $Y$ and maps $$\label{model:real-domains}
     \{Y:\Im Y<-C_\varepsilon|T|^r\}\ \longrightarrow\ \mathbb H_-.$$ It satisfies $S(0,Y)=Y$, obeys <a href="#model:energy-equation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="model:energy-equation">[model:energy-equation]</a> throughout this domain, and depends continuously on real $T$, locally uniformly on strict common interiors.*

4.  *At every strict interior point of <a href="#model:real-domains" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="model:real-domains">[model:real-domains]</a> with $T$ real, these functions have a joint holomorphic germ in $(T,Y)$. The germs agree when continued along vertical $Y$-paths from the deep model.

For every compact horizontal interval $I\subset\mathbb R$ and every $\varepsilon>0$, there is a constant $C_{I,\varepsilon}$ such that $$\label{model:power-growth}
 |F(x-\mathrm iD)|\le C_{I,\varepsilon}D^{-\lambda-\varepsilon}
 \qquad(x\in I,\ 0<D<1).$$ Equivalently, $$\label{model:log-growth}
 \sup_{x\in I}\log|F(x-\mathrm ie^{-v})|
       \le\lambda v+o(v)\qquad(v\to\infty),$$ where the error is uniform on $I$.

Let $g$ be a nontrivial meromorphic slice function, with $\log|g(y_0)|\ge-C_0$ on the real starting row. Suppose $\log|g(y_u)|\le B_0$, where $B_0\ge1$, up to a specified stopping cut $T$. Then $$\mathbb P\left\{\inf_{u\le T}\log|g(y_u)|<-B_1\right\}
     \le \frac{B_0+C_0}{B_0+B_1}
     \le\frac{C(1+B_0)}{1+B_1}
 \label{odd:log-deficit-probability}$$ for $B_1\ge1$. Earlier bounds may be incorporated by stopping at their first failure; their failure probabilities are then added separately.

With exceptions of probability tending to zero, the following hold through $\tau_L$. For every site, $$\label{sign:site-suppression}
 |s_i'|\le e^{o(h_2)-|a_i|}w_{\max}.$$ For each fixed nonempty candidate $J$, put $I=J^c$ and stop additionally when any history in $I$ reaches $2u_0$. Up to that stop, $$\label{sign:deep-controls-all}
 w_{\max}\le e^{Ch_2}\max_{j\in J}|s_j'|.$$ The exceptional probability tends to zero uniformly over the finite family of candidates. In particular, both bounds hold throughout $[0,\tau_L]$ on the final classification event with its actual sets $I,J$, apart from an event of probability tending to zero.
